insert into d (name, yr, txt) values
  ('Fall of Western Roman Empire', 476, 'In 476 the Germanic general Odoacer deposed the teenage Western Roman emperor Romulus Augustulus and sent the imperial regalia to Constantinople, choosing to rule Italy himself. By then the Western Empire had lost most of its provinces to Germanic kingdoms, so the event was more an ending than a shock. It is traditionally used to mark the end of antiquity in the West, while the Eastern Roman Empire lasted almost another thousand years.'),
  ('Battle of Zhongli', 507, 'In 507 the army of the Liang dynasty defeated a large Northern Wei force at the fortress of Zhongli on the Huai River. The fortress guarded the approaches to the southern capital Jiankang, so both sides fought hard to hold it. The victory secured Liang''s northern frontier during the long period of division between North and South China.'),
  ('Justinian''s reign', 527, 'Justinian I ruled the Byzantine Empire from 527 to 565 and tried to restore the old Roman Empire. His generals Belisarius and Narses reconquered North Africa from the Vandals and Italy from the Ostrogoths, he had Roman law gathered into the Corpus Juris Civilis, and he built the Hagia Sophia. The wars and a great plague left the empire exhausted, but his legal code shaped the law of much of Europe.'),
  ('Battle of Dara', 530, 'In June 530 the Byzantine general Belisarius defeated a larger Sasanian army at Dara, a fortress on the Persian frontier. He prepared the battlefield with trenches and hidden cavalry, and his archers and cavalry wore down the Persians. It was the first great victory of the 25-year-old commander, who went on to reconquer North Africa and Italy for Justinian.'),
  ('Battle of Satala', 530, 'In 530 a Byzantine army under Sittas defeated a Sasanian force at Satala in Armenia, in the same year as Belisarius''s victory at Dara. The two successes meant the Byzantines won on both fronts in the first campaign of the Iberian War. They helped Justinian reach the ''Eternal Peace'' of 532, which freed him to turn against the Vandals in the west.'),
  ('Battle of Ad Decimum', 533, 'On 13 September 533 Belisarius''s small Byzantine army defeated the Vandal king Gelimer at Ad Decimum, a few miles from Carthage. The Vandals had split their forces and were caught off guard, and Belisarius entered Carthage the next day. The campaign destroyed the Vandal kingdom in a matter of months and returned North Africa to Roman rule for over a century.'),
  ('Battle of Sura', 540, 'In 540 the Sasanian king Khosrow I broke the ''Eternal Peace'' and invaded Byzantine Syria, capturing and sacking the fortress city of Sura on the Euphrates before moving on to Antioch. The attack came while Justinian''s best troops were fighting in Italy. It showed how the Emperor''s western wars had left the eastern frontier exposed and began a long war with Persia.'),
  ('Sui reunifies China', 589, 'In 589 the Sui dynasty conquered the southern state of Chen and reunified China, ending about three centuries of division after the fall of the Han. Emperor Wen, the founder of the Sui, rebuilt a strong central government and the Sui started the Grand Canal linking north and south. The dynasty collapsed in 618 after costly wars, but it laid the ground for the golden age of the Tang.'),
  ('Battle of Salsu', 612, 'In 612 the Korean kingdom of Goguryeo, led by the general Eulji Mundeok, destroyed a Sui army that had crossed the Salsu River on its way to Pyongyang. Traditional accounts say that out of hundreds of thousands of invaders only a few thousand made it back. The disaster helped bring down the Sui dynasty and became a great symbol of Korean resistance to Chinese invasion.'),
  ('Tang dynasty founded', 618, 'In 618 Li Yuan, a Sui general, proclaimed himself emperor Gaozu and founded the Tang dynasty after the Sui collapsed. The Tang ruled China until 907 and presided over a golden age, with a cosmopolitan capital at Chang''an, great poets such as Li Bai and Du Fu, and busy Silk Road trade. Its power and culture influenced Korea, Japan and Vietnam.'),
  ('Hijra', 622, 'In 622 the Prophet Muhammad and his followers migrated from Mecca to Yathrib, soon renamed Medina, to escape persecution. There he built a community that became the first Islamic state. The Hijra marks the start of the Islamic calendar, whose years are counted from 622.'),
  ('Battle of the Bridge', 634, 'In 634 a Sasanian army under Bahman Jaduya defeated the Muslim Arabs at the Battle of the Bridge on the Euphrates, killing their commander Abu Ubayd. It was the only major victory of the Sasanians over the Muslim armies that were invading Persia. The Arabs returned with a larger army and won decisively at Qadisiyyah two years later.'),
  ('Battle of Qadisiyyah', 636, 'In November 636 a Muslim Arab army under Sa''d ibn Abi Waqqas defeated the Sasanian army at al-Qadisiyyah in Iraq after days of fighting, and the Persian commander Rostam Farrokhzad was killed. It was one of the most significant battles of the Muslim conquest of Persia. The Arabs soon took the capital Ctesiphon, and the Sasanian Empire never recovered.'),
  ('Conquest of Jerusalem', 638, 'Between 636 and 638 Muslim armies took Jerusalem from the Byzantine Empire after a siege of about six months. The Christian patriarch Sophronius agreed to surrender only to the Caliph Umar, who came to the city in person and guaranteed the safety of its people and churches. Jerusalem became a holy city for Islam as well as for Jews and Christians, and the Dome of the Rock was built there later in the century.'),
  ('Arab conquest of Egypt', 639, 'Between 639 and 642 the army of Amr ibn al-As conquered Byzantine Egypt for the Rashidun Caliphate. It ended about seven centuries of Roman rule that began in 30 BC. The conquerors founded a new garrison city, Fustat, a forerunner of Cairo, and over time Egypt became Arabic-speaking and largely Muslim.'),
  ('Battle of Marj Rahit', 684, 'In 684 the Umayyad army under Marwan I defeated the supporters of the rival caliph Ibn al-Zubayr at Marj Rahit near Damascus, during the Second Fitna civil war. The victory kept the Umayyad caliphate alive and passed the throne to Marwan''s branch of the family. It also deepened the rivalry between Arab tribal groups in Syria that troubled the dynasty for decades.'),
  ('Battle of Maskin', 691, 'In October 691 the Umayyad caliph Abd al-Malik defeated the forces of Mus''ab ibn al-Zubayr at Maskin, near Baghdad, and Mus''ab was killed. The victory broke the Zubayrid hold on Iraq. The Second Fitna civil war was over soon after, when Abd al-Malik''s forces took Mecca and killed Ibn al-Zubayr in 692.'),
  ('Battle of Carthage (698)', 698, 'In 698 an Umayyad army under Hasan ibn al-Nu''man captured Carthage from the Byzantines and razed it, ending Byzantine rule in North Africa. The ancient city never recovered, and the Arabs founded the new port of Tunis nearby. The conquest was a key step in the Muslim conquest of the Maghreb.'),
  ('Battle of Tours', 732, 'On 10 October 732 the Frankish leader Charles Martel defeated an Umayyad raiding army near Tours, and the Muslim commander Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi was killed. The battle is often portrayed as the moment that saved Europe from Islam, though modern historians see it as the end of a large raid rather than a full invasion. It raised Charles''s prestige and helped his family, the Carolingians, take the Frankish throne, and his grandson was Charlemagne.'),
  ('Abbasid Revolution', 750, 'In 750 the Abbasids overthrew the Umayyad caliphate with the help of a revolt that began in Khorasan in eastern Iran, and Abu al-Abbas became caliph. The Abbasids moved the capital to Baghdad, which became a centre of learning, and gave non-Arab Muslims, especially Persians, a larger role in government. Almost all of the Umayyad family was killed, but one prince escaped to Spain and founded a state at Córdoba.'),
  ('Battle of Zab', 750, 'On 25 January 750 the Abbasid army defeated the last Umayyad caliph, Marwan II, in a battle on the Great Zab River in northern Iraq. Marwan fled to Egypt and was killed there, and Umayyad rule in the east ended. The battle opened the age of the Abbasid Caliphate, which ruled from Baghdad until the Mongols arrived in 1258.'),
  ('Battle of Talas', 751, 'In July 751 the Abbasid Caliphate and its allies defeated a Tang Chinese army at the Talas River in Central Asia. It ended Tang expansion to the west and secured Abbasid control over Transoxiana. By tradition, captured Chinese prisoners passed the secrets of papermaking to the Islamic world, from which paper later spread to Europe.'),
  ('An Lushan Rebellion', 755, 'The An Lushan Rebellion of 755-763 began when the Tang general An Lushan, who commanded armies on the northeast frontier, revolted and declared his own dynasty. His forces took Luoyang and Chang''an, and the emperor fled to Sichuan. The rebellion killed millions and permanently weakened the Tang dynasty, handing real power to regional military governors.'),
  ('Battle of Yongqiu', 756, 'In 756 the Tang general Zhang Xun held the small city of Yongqiu against much larger rebel forces during the An Lushan Rebellion, and eventually defeated them. With a few thousand men he kept the rebels from advancing on the rich southeast for months. His stubborn defence made him a model of loyalty in Chinese tradition, and he died the next year defending Suiyang.'),
  ('Battle of Suiyang', 757, 'In 757 the rebel Yan army besieged Suiyang for about ten months, while Tang defenders under Zhang Xun and Xu Yuan fought on until their food ran out. The city finally fell, but the rebels were so worn down that they could not push on into the rich lands south of the Huai River. The defenders'' resolve, which reportedly went as far as cannibalism, made them symbols of loyalty and gave the Tang time to recover.'),
  ('Battle of Xiangji Temple', 757, 'In September 757 the Tang army, helped by Uighur cavalry, defeated the rebel Yan army at Xiangji Temple, near Chang''an, in a pitched battle of the An Lushan Rebellion. The victory allowed the Tang to retake the imperial capital, and the second capital Luoyang soon after. The Uighur alliance was costly, since the Tang had to pay them with the right to plunder, but it saved the dynasty for another century and a half.'),
  ('Battle of Pliska', 811, 'In July 811 the Byzantine emperor Nikephoros I invaded Bulgaria and burned its capital Pliska, but his army was then trapped in a mountain pass by Khan Krum and destroyed. Nikephoros was killed, the first Roman emperor to die in battle against a foreign enemy since Valens at Adrianople in 378. According to Byzantine writers, Krum had his skull made into a drinking cup.'),
  ('Battle of Ashdown', 871, 'In about January 871 the West Saxons under King Æthelred and his younger brother Alfred defeated a Danish Viking army at Ashdown on the Berkshire Downs. Alfred is credited with the decisive attack, which came before his brother had finished praying. Æthelred died a few months later and Alfred became king, going on to save Wessex from the Vikings and to be remembered as Alfred the Great.'),
  ('Battle of Brenta', 899, 'On 24 September 899 Hungarian horsemen crushed the army of King Berengar I of Italy at the Brenta River, using fast light cavalry and feigned retreats. It was one of the earliest battles of the Hungarian invasions of Europe. The victory opened northern Italy to Hungarian raids, which went on for decades until their defeat at Lechfeld.'),
  ('Battle of Lechfeld', 955, 'From 10 to 12 August 955 the German king Otto I destroyed a large Hungarian army near Augsburg at the Lechfeld, after the Hungarians had raided Germany for decades. It ended the Hungarian raids on Western Europe, and the Hungarians then settled down and became Christian, founding the Kingdom of Hungary around the year 1000. The prestige of the victory helped Otto to be crowned Holy Roman Emperor in 962.'),
  ('Song dynasty founded', 960, 'In 960 the general Zhao Kuangyin took the throne in a coup, became Emperor Taizu and founded the Song dynasty, ending the chaos of the Five Dynasties period. Under the Song China saw great economic growth and inventions such as movable-type printing, paper money and gunpowder weapons, and the civil service examinations were expanded. Its military was weak, however, and it lost the north to the Jurchen and finally the whole country to the Mongols.'),
  ('Founding of Cairo', 969, 'In 969 the Fatimid general Jawhar al-Siqilli founded Cairo, ''the Victorious'', as the new capital of the Fatimid caliphate after conquering Egypt for it. The caliph moved there soon afterwards, and the al-Azhar mosque, begun in 970, grew into one of the world''s oldest centres of learning. Cairo went on to become the largest city in Africa and the Arab world.'),
  ('Battle of Kleidion', 1014, 'On 29 July 1014 the Byzantine emperor Basil II trapped and destroyed the army of the Bulgarian tsar Samuel in the Kleidion pass in the Belasitsa mountains. According to tradition Basil had thousands of prisoners blinded, leaving one man in a hundred with one eye to lead the rest home, and Samuel died of shock soon afterwards. Bulgaria was completely conquered by 1018, and Basil became known as the ''Bulgar-slayer''.'),
  ('Great Schism', 1054, 'In 1054 a papal legate and the Patriarch of Constantinople excommunicated each other, a dramatic climax to centuries of disputes between the Latin West and the Greek East. The quarrels were over the authority of the pope, the Filioque clause in the creed and other matters of practice. The rupture became permanent, dividing Christianity into the Catholic and Eastern Orthodox churches, and the mutual excommunications were only lifted in 1965.'),
  ('Battle of Hastings', 1066, 'On 14 October 1066 William, Duke of Normandy, defeated the English king Harold Godwinson at Hastings and began the Norman conquest of England. Harold was killed after a long day of fighting, traditionally by an arrow in the eye, and William was crowned king on Christmas Day. The conquest changed English law, language and landholding, replacing the Anglo-Saxon nobility with a Norman-French elite, and the Bayeux Tapestry tells its story.'),
  ('Battle of Stamford Bridge', 1066, 'On 25 September 1066 King Harold Godwinson surprised and crushed a Norwegian invasion army at Stamford Bridge near York, where King Harald Hardrada and Harold''s own brother Tostig were killed. It is often seen as the end of the Viking Age in England. But three days later William of Normandy landed in the south, and Harold''s tired army had to march some 250 miles to face him at Hastings.'),
  ('Norman Conquest of England', 1066, 'The Norman Conquest began in 1066 when William of Normandy won the English crown at Hastings, claiming that the childless Edward the Confessor had promised it to him. William''s Normans built castles across the land, put down revolts brutally, and replaced the old English nobility with their own followers. The conquest changed England''s language, government and culture, and the Domesday Book of 1086 recorded who owned what in the new kingdom.'),
  ('Battle of Manzikert', 1071, 'On 26 August 1071 the Seljuk sultan Alp Arslan crushed the Byzantine army at Manzikert in eastern Anatolia and captured the emperor Romanos IV Diogenes. The defeat led to civil war in the Byzantine Empire and opened Anatolia to Turkish settlement. It started the long decline of Byzantine power in Asia Minor and it helped bring about the Crusades.'),
  ('Battle of Sagrajas', 1086, 'On 23 October 1086 the Almoravid ruler Yusuf ibn Tashfin crossed from North Africa at the request of Muslim rulers in Spain and defeated Alfonso VI of Castile at Sagrajas, near Badajoz. Alfonso had just taken Toledo and was threatening the divided Muslim taifa kingdoms. The victory halted the Christian advance for a time, but the Almoravids soon took over the taifas themselves.'),
  ('Battle of Civetot', 1096, 'On 21 October 1096 a Seljuk Turkish army destroyed the People''s Crusade, a poorly armed and undisciplined force of peasants and some knights following Peter the Hermit, at Civetot in Anatolia. Most of the crusaders were killed or enslaved, and the few survivors joined the larger First Crusade later. The disaster showed the dangers of fighting in Anatolia without trained knights and supplies.'),
  ('First Crusade', 1096, 'In 1096 the first great armies set out for the Holy Land after Pope Urban II called for a crusade at Clermont in 1095. After years of hardship they took Nicaea, Antioch and finally Jerusalem in 1099, and founded the Crusader states of Edessa, Antioch and Jerusalem. It was the first of a series of crusades that shaped relations between Europe and the Middle East for centuries.'),
  ('Battle of Antioch', 1098, 'On 28 June 1098 the starving Crusaders marched out of Antioch and defeated the larger relief army of Kerbogha, the ruler of Mosul, in a battle outside the walls. Kerbogha''s coalition fell apart because of quarrels among its commanders. The victory saved the First Crusade, which was close to destruction, and left Bohemond of Taranto in control of Antioch as a Crusader prince.'),
  ('Siege of Antioch', 1098, 'From October 1097 to June 1098 the Crusaders besieged the heavily fortified city of Antioch, suffering terrible hunger and disease. They finally got in on 3 June when an Armenian captain in the Seljuk garrison let them into a tower. Almost at once they were besieged themselves by Kerbogha''s relief army, but the discovery of the ''Holy Lance'' raised their spirits and they won the battle that followed.'),
  ('Battle of Ascalon', 1099, 'On 12 August 1099, weeks after taking Jerusalem, the Crusaders under Godfrey of Bouillon surprised and defeated a Fatimid army from Egypt near Ascalon. It is often considered the last action of the First Crusade. The victory secured the new Kingdom of Jerusalem, though quarrels among the Crusader leaders allowed Ascalon itself to stay in Fatimid hands for decades.'),
  ('Capture of Jerusalem by Crusaders', 1099, 'On 15 July 1099 the Crusaders stormed Jerusalem after a siege of five weeks and massacred many of its Muslim and Jewish inhabitants. The conquest was the high point of the First Crusade, whose aim had been to recover the Holy Sepulchre from Muslim rule. It created the Kingdom of Jerusalem, which survived until Saladin''s reconquest of the city in 1187.'),
  ('Siege of Jerusalem', 1099, 'The Crusaders reached Jerusalem on 7 June 1099 and, short of food and water, built wooden siege towers using timber brought from the coast. In the final assault in mid-July they stormed the walls from a tower and then took the city. The fall of Jerusalem ended the First Crusade, and for Western Christians it was the sign that God favoured the venture.'),
  ('Battle of Montgisard', 1177, 'On 25 November 1177 the 16-year-old king Baldwin IV of Jerusalem, who suffered from leprosy, led a small Crusader army to a surprise victory over Saladin''s much larger force at Montgisard near Ramla. Saladin barely escaped back to Egypt. It was one of the most remarkable Crusader victories, and it delayed Saladin''s attack on the kingdom for about ten years.'),
  ('Battle of Hattin', 1187, 'On 4 July 1187 Saladin destroyed the Crusader army of King Guy of Lusignan at the Horns of Hattin in Galilee, after leading it into waterless country in the summer heat. Most of the knights were killed or captured, the True Cross was taken, and Raynald of Châtillon was executed. Without an army, the Crusader states could not hold their cities, and Jerusalem fell to Saladin in October, which led to the Third Crusade.'),
  ('Battle of Arsuf', 1191, 'On 7 September 1191 Richard the Lionheart defeated Saladin''s army outside Arsuf as the Crusaders marched along the coast from Acre toward Jaffa. Richard held his men in tight order under harassing fire until the moment came for a general counterattack. It was the Third Crusade''s biggest pitched battle and showed that Saladin could be beaten, though it did not bring the Crusaders any nearer to retaking Jerusalem.'),
  ('Battle of Jaffa', 1192, 'In August 1192 Richard the Lionheart relieved the besieged port of Jaffa with a handful of knights and fought in the surf, then held the city against Saladin''s much larger army. It was the last battle of the Third Crusade. Soon afterwards the two leaders made a truce that left the Crusaders the coastal strip and allowed Christian pilgrims to visit Jerusalem.'),
  ('Battle of Alarcos', 1195, 'On 18 July 1195 the Almohad caliph Abu Yusuf Ya''qub al-Mansur crushed the Castilian army of Alfonso VIII at Alarcos in central Spain. Alfonso had attacked without waiting for his allies, and he fled to Toledo with heavy losses. The defeat set back the Reconquista, but it also pushed the Christian kingdoms to unite, leading to the great victory at Las Navas de Tolosa in 1212.'),
  ('Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa', 1212, 'On 16 July 1212 the combined armies of Castile, Aragon and Navarre, joined by crusaders from across Europe, defeated the Almohad caliph al-Nasir at Las Navas de Tolosa in southern Spain. It was the decisive turning point of the Reconquista and broke the power of the Almohads. Within decades the Christians took Córdoba and Seville, leaving only Granada as a Muslim state.'),
  ('Battle of Muret', 1213, 'On 12 September 1213 a small crusader army under Simon de Montfort defeated a much larger force of King Peter II of Aragon and his Occitan allies at Muret, near Toulouse, and Peter was killed. It was the last major battle of the Albigensian Crusade, which was fought against the Cathars of southern France. The defeat ended Aragon''s power north of the Pyrenees and opened the south of France to the French crown.'),
  ('Magna Carta', 1215, 'On 15 June 1215 King John of England sealed Magna Carta at Runnymede, forced by rebel barons to accept limits on his power. It promised that the king, too, was bound by law and that free men could not be punished without lawful judgment. The pope soon annulled it and war followed, but later kings reissued it, and it became a lasting symbol of the rule of law that influenced the American Constitution.'),
  ('Battle of Kalka River', 1223, 'On 31 May 1223 a Mongol army under Jebe and Subutai crushed the combined armies of the Rus'' princes and the Cumans at the Kalka River, in what is now Ukraine. It was the first encounter between the Mongols and the Rus'', and the princes'' lack of coordination contributed to the disaster. The Mongols then withdrew, but they returned fourteen years later under Batu Khan and conquered Rus''.'),
  ('Battle of Mohi', 1241, 'On 11 April 1241 the Mongol army under Batu Khan and Subutai destroyed the Hungarian army of King Béla IV at Mohi, after a surprise night crossing of the Sajó River and a bombardment with catapults. Hungary was devastated, and a large part of its population died in the following year. The Mongols left only because of the death of the Great Khan Ögedei, and Béla then rebuilt Hungary with stone castles.'),
  ('Mongol sack of Baghdad', 1258, 'In February 1258 the Mongol army of Hulagu captured and sacked Baghdad, the capital of the Abbasid Caliphate for 500 years, and killed the caliph al-Musta''sim. Tens of thousands, perhaps far more, of its people were killed, and many libraries and scholarly treasures were destroyed. It ended the Abbasid caliphate in Baghdad and is seen as a crushing blow to the Islamic Golden Age.'),
  ('Siege of Baghdad', 1258, 'Hulagu''s Mongol army, with Georgian and Armenian allies, surrounded Baghdad in late January 1258 and bombarded the walls with siege engines. After about two weeks the Caliph surrendered, and the Mongols spent a week sacking the city. The siege showed the Mongols'' skill in siege warfare and ended the political power of the Abbasid caliphs.'),
  ('Battle of Ain Jalut', 1260, 'On 3 September 1260 the Egyptian Mamluks under Sultan Qutuz and Baibars defeated a Mongol army at Ain Jalut in Galilee. It was the first major defeat of the Mongols in a pitched battle and stopped their advance into Egypt and North Africa. The Mamluks became the leading power of the Muslim world, and Baibars soon became sultan.'),
  ('Siege of Aleppo', 1260, 'In January 1260 the Mongols under Hulagu, helped by Armenian and Frankish allies, besieged Aleppo and took it after a week, killing many of its people. The city fell because its defenders could not hold out against the Mongol siege engines. The disaster showed how complete the Mongol conquest of Syria was, and Damascus surrendered soon after.'),
  ('Siege of Damascus', 1260, 'In March 1260 Damascus, the great Syrian city, surrendered to the Mongol general Kitbuqa, who was a Christian, and the Armenian and Frankish rulers who were allied with the Mongols entered the city. It looked as if the Mongols and the Crusaders might destroy Muslim power in the Middle East. Hulagu then left for Persia, and the Mamluks defeated Kitbuqa at Ain Jalut in September.'),
  ('Mongol conquest of Song', 1271, 'In 1271 Kublai Khan proclaimed the Yuan dynasty, announcing the Mongol conquest of China, which was still resisted by the Southern Song. The Mongols took the great Song city of Xiangyang in 1273 and destroyed the last Song resistance at the sea battle of Yamen in 1279. It was the first time all of China was ruled by a foreign dynasty, and Kublai united China under the Yuan.'),
  ('Yuan dynasty founded', 1271, 'In 1271 Kublai Khan, a grandson of Genghis Khan, named his regime the Great Yuan and made the city of Dadu, today''s Beijing, its capital. The Yuan dynasty ruled China until 1368 and was the first time the whole country came under foreign rule. Its Mongol rulers used Chinese officials but kept Mongols at the top of society, and Venetian traveller Marco Polo visited Kublai''s court.'),
  ('Battle of Elbistan', 1277, 'On 15 April 1277 the Mamluk sultan Baybars led his army deep into Anatolia and defeated a Mongol force with its Seljuk allies at Elbistan. The Mongols did well at first, but the larger Mamluk army won the day. It was the Mamluks'' furthest advance into Anatolia and limited Mongol influence there, but Baybars withdrew soon afterwards and died later that year.'),
  ('Battle of Acre', 1291, 'On 18 May 1291 the Mamluk sultan al-Ashraf Khalil took Acre, the last major Crusader stronghold in the Holy Land, after a siege of about six weeks. Many of the defenders and inhabitants were killed or enslaved, and the other Crusader cities fell or were abandoned within weeks. The loss ended the Crusaders'' presence on the mainland, nearly two centuries after the First Crusade.'),
  ('Battle of Stirling Bridge', 1297, 'On 11 September 1297 the Scots under Andrew Moray and William Wallace defeated a much larger English army at Stirling Bridge on the River Forth. The English were forced to cross the narrow bridge in small groups, and the Scots cut them off and destroyed the vanguard. It was the first major Scottish victory of the Wars of Independence and made Wallace one of Scotland''s national heroes.'),
  ('Battle of Falkirk', 1298, 'On 22 July 1298 King Edward I of England defeated William Wallace''s Scottish army at Falkirk. English longbowmen shot down the tightly packed Scottish spearmen, who had stood firm against cavalry, in an early demonstration of the longbow''s power. Wallace resigned as Guardian of Scotland soon afterwards, and he was captured and executed in 1305.'),
  ('Battle of Wadi al-Khazandar', 1299, 'In December 1299 a Mongol army of the Ilkhanate under Ghazan defeated the Mamluks near Homs, in the third battle fought there. The Mongols briefly occupied Damascus, but they soon withdrew, and the Mamluks recovered Syria. It was the last big Mongol victory in Syria, and after the Mamluk victory at Marj al-Saffar in 1303 the Mongols never again seriously threatened it.'),
  ('Battle of Bannockburn', 1314, 'On 23-24 June 1314 Robert the Bruce''s army defeated a much larger English army under Edward II at Bannockburn, near Stirling. The Scottish spearmen held a strong position among marshy ground and streams where the English cavalry could not manoeuvre. It was a decisive victory in the First War of Scottish Independence, which ended with English recognition of Scotland''s independence in 1328, and it remains a symbol of Scottish national pride.'),
  ('Mansa Musa''s pilgrimage', 1324, 'In 1324 Mansa Musa, the ruler of the Mali Empire, travelled to Mecca on pilgrimage with a huge caravan carrying large amounts of gold. In Cairo he gave away so much of it that the price of gold fell for years. His journey put Mali on the maps of Europe and the Islamic world and made him a legend as one of the richest people in history; he also returned with scholars and architects who helped make Timbuktu a centre of learning.'),
  ('Hundred Years'' War', 1337, 'From 1337 to 1453 England and France fought the Hundred Years'' War over the English kings'' claim to the French throne and over the duchy of Aquitaine. It saw the English longbow triumph at Crécy, Poitiers and Agincourt, the plague, and Joan of Arc''s rise, before France finally won and England kept only Calais. The war strengthened national feeling in both countries and changed the nature of armies, as cannons and professional soldiers became more important.'),
  ('Battle of Crécy', 1346, 'On 26 August 1346 Edward III''s smaller English army defeated King Philip VI''s French army at Crécy in northern France. English longbowmen cut down wave after wave of French knights and Genoese crossbowmen, and many of the French nobility died. The battle showed that the longbow and disciplined infantry could beat the mounted knight, and it led to the English capture of Calais.'),
  ('Black Death reaches Europe', 1347, 'In October 1347 the Black Death reached Europe, probably brought to Sicily by Genoese ships from the Black Sea. The plague spread over the continent in a few years and killed an estimated third to half of the population. The huge loss of life made workers scarce, raising wages and weakening serfdom, and it left deep marks on European society, religion and art.'),
  ('Battle of Poitiers', 1356, 'On 19 September 1356 Edward the Black Prince''s small Anglo-Gascon army defeated a much larger French army near Poitiers and captured King John II. English archers again broke the French attacks, and the French nobility was left in disarray. The huge ransom for the king and the peace of 1360 gave England large territories in France, but they were soon lost again.'),
  ('Ming dynasty founded', 1368, 'In 1368 the rebel leader Zhu Yuanzhang, born a poor peasant, drove the Mongols out of Nanjing and proclaimed himself the Hongwu Emperor, founding the Ming dynasty. The Ming restored Han Chinese rule after almost a century of Mongol rule and lasted until 1644. Its achievements include the Forbidden City, the Great Wall as we know it today, and the voyages of Zheng He.'),
  ('Battle of Kosovo', 1389, 'On 15 June 1389 an Ottoman army under Sultan Murad I fought a Serbian-led coalition under Prince Lazar at Kosovo Polje, and both leaders died, Murad in his own camp and Lazar by execution. The result was probably a bloody draw, but the Ottomans were stronger and Serbia soon became their vassal. The battle became the central legend in Serbian national memory and is remembered each year on Vidovdan.'),
  ('Battle of Nicopolis', 1396, 'On 25 September 1396 the Ottoman sultan Bayezid I destroyed a large Crusader army at Nicopolis on the Danube. The French knights charged recklessly against the advice of their Hungarian allies and were cut down, and Bayezid executed many prisoners. It was one of the last major Crusades, and it confirmed Ottoman control of the Balkans.'),
  ('Battle of Ankara', 1402, 'On 28 July 1402 the Central Asian conqueror Timur destroyed the army of the Ottoman sultan Bayezid I at Ankara and captured him. The defeat threw the Ottoman state into civil war among Bayezid''s sons for eleven years. It gave the Byzantine Empire a half-century of extra life before Constantinople fell in 1453.'),
  ('Zheng He''s voyages', 1405, 'Between 1405 and 1433 the Ming admiral Zheng He led seven great naval expeditions with huge fleets of hundreds of ships to Southeast Asia, India, Arabia and East Africa. The giant treasure ships carried tens of thousands of men and were far larger than anything Europe had at the time. The voyages displayed Ming power and brought tribute, but they were stopped for their cost, and China turned inward.'),
  ('Battle of Grunwald', 1410, 'On 15 July 1410 the combined armies of Poland and Lithuania, led by King Jogaila and Grand Duke Vytautas, crushed the Teutonic Order at Grunwald. The Grand Master, Ulrich von Jungingen, was killed along with most of the Order''s leadership. It broke the military power of the Teutonic Knights and made the Polish-Lithuanian union the main power of eastern Europe, and it is still a great national symbol in Poland and Lithuania.'),
  ('Battle of Tannenberg', 1410, 'On 15 July 1410 a Polish-Lithuanian army defeated the Teutonic Knights in the great battle that the Germans call Tannenberg, after a nearby village, and the Poles and Lithuanians call Grunwald and Žalgiris. The Order''s Grand Master was killed, and its power in the Baltic never recovered. The name Tannenberg was later reused for a German victory over Russia in 1914, which the Germans presented as revenge for 1410.'),
  ('Battle of Agincourt', 1415, 'On 25 October 1415, St Crispin''s Day, Henry V''s exhausted and much smaller English army defeated a large French army at Agincourt in northern France. The French knights had to attack across a muddy field and were shot down by English longbowmen, and many French nobles were killed or captured. The victory led to the Treaty of Troyes in 1420, which made Henry heir to the French throne, and Shakespeare later made it famous in his play Henry V.'),
  ('Rise of the Aztec Triple Alliance', 1428, 'In 1428 the city-states of Tenochtitlan, Texcoco and Tlacopan formed the Triple Alliance and overthrew the dominant Tepanec city of Azcapotzalco, and so began the Aztec Empire. The alliance conquered much of central Mexico and collected tribute from the conquered peoples, which fed the great capital Tenochtitlan. It lasted until the Spanish conquest in 1521, when the resentful subject peoples helped Cortés destroy it.'),
  ('Battle of Varna', 1444, 'On 10 November 1444 the Ottoman sultan Murad II defeated a Crusader army led by King Władysław III of Poland and Hungary and the Hungarian general John Hunyadi at Varna in Bulgaria. The young king was killed in a reckless charge against the sultan''s guard. It was the last big crusade to try to drive the Ottomans out of Europe, and Constantinople fell nine years later.'),
  ('Gutenberg printing revolution', 1450, 'Around 1450 Johannes Gutenberg of Mainz developed a printing press using movable metal type and oil-based ink, and his 42-line Bible appeared around 1455. Printing spread across Europe within decades, making books far cheaper and ideas easier to spread. It helped drive the Renaissance, the Reformation and the Scientific Revolution, and it is often named as the most important invention of the second millennium.');
